patch.body:
Kleiburg was finished in 1971: a bent slab of about 500 rented flats, some 400 m long and ten storeys high, part of the honeycomb plan that Siegfried Nassuth of the city planning department drew for the Bijlmermeer. Like its neighbours it had a raised indoor street linked to a car park, storage boxes on the ground floor and long open galleries. In the 1980s it got cameras, sturdier doors and extra lifts, but drugs, vacancy and crime stayed. On 4 October 1992 the El Al cargo plane came down on the neighbouring flats, and Kleiburg was evacuated. A planned showpiece renovation around 2000, with lofts, escalators and a wavy new façade, was dropped for lack of money.

In 2009 housing corporation Rochdale began moving residents out to demolish it. Local campaigners pushed back, and Rochdale sold the building for a symbolic one euro to a consortium with a credible plan. The consortium, DeFlat, repaired the structure, galleries, lifts and façade, and sold the flats as bare shells (*klushuizen*, do-it-yourself homes) that buyers finished themselves, combining units if they liked. The shells went for an extremely low price and sold fast; by the end of 2014 the building was saved, and the renovation was finished in December 2016. In 2017 NL Architects and XVW architectuur won the EU Mies van der Rohe Award for it, the first building in the Netherlands to win it.

Look for:
- the sheer length of the bent slab, some 400 metres;
- the new ground floor, where the storage boxes made way for homes, studios and entrances;
- the variety of window frames, picked by individual owners from a catalogue.
